                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I started off Avalon Legends Solitaire sharing a lot of the detractors' sentiments, but once I got past my preconceptions of what a solitaire game should be, I found myself enjoying the gameplay so much more than I expected. No, this isn't another \"gotta 100% every level\" game, but it's exactly that angle that makes it so unique and interesting to me. And let's be honest, the genre could do with a little more creativity these days.\n\nEach of Avalon's levels is comprised of 10 boards with specific goals to meet in order to progress. These goals come in 3 varieties: get X number of perfects, earn X number of points, or get a combo of X number of cards. Naturally, the further you get into into the game, the more difficult the goals get. And as the goals ramp up, so does the need for strategy, and that's when my enjoyment increased exponentially. Once I let go of my need to fully clear each board, I started making calls on whether I should blow all my Wild Cards on a possible chain, or call a board a loss and move on. Did I have enough in my arsenal to pick up the number of perfects I needed to clear the stage, or should I call it a wash and start banking those Wild Cards for my next push? \n\nThe game is clearly an older port, however, and the UI is fairly bare bones and missing much of the polish that one would expect of any self-respecting casual title. There's really no reason you can't view the stage goals/progress while on a board, nor is it excusable to only be able to view the collectibles/awards screen between each tableau. And just why can't I open up the Power-Up Shop from the main menu? But as frustrating as these issues were, I still found myself engaged enough to overlook them as I continued to push through to the end.\n\nAvalon Legends of Solitaire may not be the greatest solitaire game I have ever played, but it's certainly a pleasant surprise amid an ocean of cookie-cutter titles. Unfortunately, it seems the sequel drops the level requirements for a more conventional \"perfect each stage\" approach, which is disappointing. I would love to see an evolution of this formula, but I guess I'll need to be on the lookout for it elsewhere.\n\n[code][h1]Curated by: [url=https://store.steampowered.com/curator/33004802-Chudah%27s-Corner/]Chudah's Corner[/url][/h1][/code]",
